  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1216 PM MST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nIt's a cool and blustery day across the region in the wake of last\nnight's system. The whole area remains under cyclonic flow aloft\nas an upper level trough rolls across northern New Mexico. This is\nleading to west winds gusting 30 to 35 mph across the area, and\nthe odd gust to 40 is possible before sunset. Mountain wave clouds\nare bringing partly cloudy skies to the lowlands, with more\nextensive orographic cloud cover over the higher terrain of the\nGila region and the Sacramento Mountains. A few light showers have\nbeen cropping up as expected, and a few snow flakes have been\nspotted around Cloudcroft, where temperatures are in the mid-30s\nthis afternoon.\n\nWest winds will east up tonight as the pressure gradient weakens,\nand skies clear out. Patchy fog is possible in some of the more\nsheltered valleys. Elsewhere, we'll have just enough wind to\nprevent more widespread fog formation. Temperatures will drop into\nthe upper-30s in some of the sheltered valleys as well. \n\nExpect less cloud cover and wind on Sunday, with temperatures\nclimbing a little warmer in the higher terrain. Calmer conditions\nSunday night will allow temperatures to fall deeper into the 30s\nin the usual cooler spots in the lowland deserts, with mid to\nupper-30s possible in the lowest-lying portions of the Mesilla\nValley. We could push the freezing mark at Jornada Range. \n\nEasterly flow will keep temperatures at or slightly below normal\nearly next week, with a warm up late-week allowing highs to\napproach the lower-70s.",
